# The image needs requirements.lock, src/reporting_agent/ and themes/ — and nothing else.
# Without this, a local .venv ships ~190 MB of context to the daemon on every build.
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.hypothesis/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
*.md
.python-version

# The dev closure (pytest, hypothesis, ruff) must never reach the image. The Dockerfile
# names requirements.lock exactly, so this is belt-and-braces — but this file works by
# exclusion, so anything not listed here IS in the build context, and a future
# `COPY requirements*.lock ./` would otherwise silently pull the dev tools in.
requirements-dev.lock
# ...except the Metric Definitions evidence, which is the one thing under tests/ that the
# BUILD needs (Req 2.6). The Catalog_Evidence_Guard compares every catalog entry against
# the recorded response it was derived from, and it runs in the image build as well as in
# the suite — a guard that only ran in the suite could not stop an image carrying an entry
# the evidence beside it contradicts, and a guessed metric name is permanently
# uncollectable with nothing failing at run time.
#
# Re-included rather than duplicated into src/. Two copies of the evidence is the worst
# available option: they can disagree about what Azure reported, and then neither is
# evidence. These are recorded API responses with every subscription id, tenant id,
# resource id and credential-shaped value excluded — asserted by the guard itself, not
# assumed — so nothing sensitive travels with them.
#
# `!` exceptions are last-match-wins **per path**, so the exclusion above is written as
# `tests/**` (every file under tests/) rather than `tests/` (the directory itself). That
# distinction is the whole trick: excluding the directory makes its contents unreachable no
# matter what follows, while excluding its files leaves a later `!` free to re-include
# exactly the ones the build needs.
#
# Verified by building the context and listing what arrived, not by reading the docs —
# tests/test_image_build.py asserts the two patterns are present and in this order.
tests/**
!tests/fixtures/metric_definitions/**
